      "title": "Tehran Municipality: Administration and Services | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Tehran Municipality's district administration, planning history, waste-management responsibilities, and the limits of available performance evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "city-and-districts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Administering the capital at different scales",
        "content": "Tehran Municipality is the executive organization responsible for municipal affairs in the capital. Research on the city's planning describes a citywide mayor selected by the city council, with district mayors appointed and supervised by the Tehran mayor. District administration brings operational management closer to different parts of a very large city; it is not a separate system of directly elected district mayors. [s1]\n\nThe same study describes 22 municipal districts. This is a documented configuration in the historical research, rather than a freshly verified administrative inventory for the editorial cutoff. It also distinguishes municipal management from national and territorial government, whose institutions participate in planning decisions. The municipality is therefore an important urban actor without being the sole authority over the capital's development. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "planning-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From physical plans to strategic ambitions",
        "content": "The planning study traces a move from predominantly physical development plans toward strategic approaches concerned with resources, pollution, transport, administrative efficiency, and public space. Its account of the Tehran 2025 vision describes an effort to combine economic, social, and environmental objectives through professional and institutional participation. These are historical planning ambitions; the passage of the target year does not establish their achievement. [s1]\n\nThis distinction is central to reading municipal documents: a vision explains intended direction, while implementation needs separate evidence.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "waste-management",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Waste management as an institutional case study",
        "content": "Waste provides a concrete example of municipal work extending beyond street collection. UCLG-MEWA, a regional association of local governments, reports that Tehran began mechanizing collection in 2007 and subsequently emphasized waste reduction, separation at source, and recycling. Its 2023 notice presents the development of a strategic waste-management document, demonstrating a planning initiative rather than independently measured results. [s2]\n\nA case study hosted by Metropolis describes the Waste Reduction Plan, known as KAP, developed by Tehran Municipality's Waste Management Organization. Its scope covers storage, collection, transfer, processing, public information, and monitoring. This makes clear that municipal waste management is a chain of connected operations: better collection alone cannot demonstrate better final disposal. [s3]\n\nThe case study lists objectives including cleaner collection, improved recycling, cost management, and greater transparency. Those stated objectives are useful evidence of program design. Its reported completion label does not independently establish durable environmental benefits, and the page contains an inconsistent district count that is excluded from this account. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-results",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading claims about municipal performance",
        "content": "For readers assessing Tehran's administration, the practical distinction is between organizational responsibility, a proposed intervention, and a demonstrated outcome. The inspected sources establish a district-based management model and documented planning efforts. They do not provide a consistent recent annual account of delivery across the city. A responsible assessment should retain that boundary rather than turning program descriptions into a performance ranking. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
